// Jahshaka fork ad452604a+155a56bf8+0338ca7f2+c4c80b5f7 (was 0065) — THE ORDER-INDEPENDENT VOXEL MERGE (Voxelizer_piece_cs).
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. `VctVoxelizer::build()` issues one compute dispatch per bucket
// per octant, and upstream merged each dispatch's triangles into the RGBA8 voxel
// textures as a RUNNING MEAN (`mixAverage3/4`): load the mean written so far,
// weight it by the count, add this dispatch's, store the new mean back — 8-bit,
// once per dispatch. An average of the same numbers taken in a different order
// is the same value only up to that quantisation, so WHICH INSTANCES LANDED IN
// WHICH DISPATCH decided the picture, by a few 8-bit steps per voxel and by
// ~6/255 on screen after the bounce. That is why fork ad452604a+155a56bf8 (was 0062) had to give every
// MATERIAL its own bucket (one dispatch per material makes the partition a
// property of the scene rather than of the attach order) — and why the outermost
// cascade of a scene whose 8,001 primitives each own a material cost 321 ms
// instead of 86: a dispatch is sized by the whole octant however few instances
// it holds.
//
// THE FIX IS TO STOP MERGING MEANS. Every contribution is snapped to a fixed
// point grid and added into a per-voxel INTEGER SUM; the mean is computed ONCE,
// by the resolve pass, after every dispatch has run. Integer addition is exact
// and associative, so the voxels do not depend on the partition at all, and a
// bucket can go back to being a material POOL.
//
// THE GRID. 12 fractional bits (1/4096 = 2.4e-4) — 6 % of one step of the 8-bit
// texture the mean is finally written to, so the snap is invisible — and one
// contribution is clamped to [0, 16] first, so a contribution is at most
// 16 * 4096 = 65,536 and 65,535 of them are 4,294,901,760: the WHOLE uint32 bar
// one contribution, not half of it. The triangle counter is NOT a 16-bit cap
// either — the packing that would give it one is the HLSL `!typed_uav_load`
// branch alone, and on Vulkan `voxelAccumVal` is an R32_UINT counting freely —
// so the albedo and emissive sums are added with SATURATION rather than left to
// wrap: a voxel with more than 65,535 clamped contributions (a dense mesh inside
// a far cascade's cell) stops at 0xFFFFFFFF and resolves to a white voxel
// instead of wrapping to a dark one. Saturating addition is still commutative
// and associative — once the ceiling is reached it stays there — so the merge is
// exactly as order-independent as it was.

@piece( DeclVoxelMerge )

#define VOXEL_MERGE_SCALE 4096.0f
#define VOXEL_MERGE_CLAMP 16.0f

	/// ONE VOXEL'S EIGHTEEN ACCUMULATOR TEXELS ARE ADJACENT IN Z: albedo sum at
	/// z*18+0..3 (rgba), RAW normal sum at +4..6 (xyz, two's complement),
	/// emissive sum at +7..9 (rgb), FOLDED normal sum at +10..12 (xyz), the
	/// ROUGHNESS sum at +13 (PHOTON-WRITER-1), the PER-HALF-AXIS COVERAGE sums
	/// packed into +14 (the faces looking +x, +y, +z) and +15 (looking -x, -y, -z)
	/// (PHOTON-VOXEL-3/-4, THE DIRECTIONAL COVERAGE below) and the matching
	/// POSITION-WEIGHTED sums into +16 and +17 (PHOTON-VOXEL-4, THE SURFACE POSITION
	/// below). Eighteen because only eighteen carry anything — a fourth
	/// channel on the three vectors would be 25 MB read and written for nothing
	/// at 128^3.
	/// Interleaved rather than stacked planes so the shader needs no knowledge of
	/// the volume's depth (a voxelisation dispatch covers one OCTANT and does not
	/// know it), and so a voxel's texels share a cache line's worth of
	/// the 3D swizzle.
	///
	/// R32_UINT AND NOT RGBA32_UINT, AND THE REASON IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT A HANG
	/// (VOXMERGE-2, 2026-09-18 — the patch header carries both tables). It used
	/// to be a hang: clearing a PFG_RGBA32_UINT 3D uav through
	/// `ComputeTools::clearUavUint` lost the device 8 runs of 8 on the Showroom
	/// samples. fork b028638c1 (was 0067) then found that hazard's real mechanism (a
	/// device-local block recycled one frame early; the whole-volume clear was
	/// merely the first write into it) and fork ad452604a+0338ca7f2+c4c80b5f7 (was 0071) stopped this accumulator
	/// being recycled at all, and on that stack the 128-bit clear is CLEAN —
	/// 16 rounds of 16 with zero Xid, against 16 of 16 for this layout. What
	/// decides it now is cost, and a full RGBA32 arm measured the SAME GPU time
	/// per rebuild on the 8,026-instance lattice (the merge touches only voxels a
	/// dispatch contributed to, and the CLEAR — the part that touches every voxel
	/// — writes 64 bytes per voxel that way against 52 here) while holding 12 MB
	/// more per 64^3 volume, resident.
	INLINE uint3 voxelMergeUvw( uint3 voxelCel, uint chan )
	{
		return uint3( voxelCel.x, voxelCel.y, voxelCel.z * 18u + chan );
	}

	/// THE TWO NORMAL SUMS, AND WHY THERE ARE TWO.
	///
	/// Upstream decided "this voxel holds surfaces facing opposite ways" WHILE
	/// summing: it compared each triangle's normal against the sum SO FAR, and if
	/// the angle exceeded 120 degrees it set the double-sided flag and dropped
	/// that triangle from the sum. That is first-one-wins — the answer depends on
	/// which triangle the thread reached first, which is the instance order in
	/// the dispatch, which is the attach order. It is the LAST order dependence
	/// in the voxelisation once the merge itself is exact, and it is worth 6/255
	/// on gi.cascade_determinism's room.
	///
	/// So the decision moves to the resolve pass, where it is made from two sums
	/// that do not care about order: the RAW sum of every normal, and the sum of
	/// every normal FOLDED into one half-space. `voxelMergeCanonical` folds by
	/// the sign of the normal's largest component, which is arbitrary but has the
	/// one property the test needs — ANTIPODAL NORMALS ALWAYS FOLD TOGETHER, since
	/// sign(-n) = -sign(n). Two surfaces facing opposite ways therefore cancel in
	/// the raw sum and reinforce in the folded one.
	///
	/// AND THE TWO SUMS GIVE BACK THE TWO GROUPS. Folded = U - F and raw = U + F,
	/// where U is the sum of the normals the fold left alone and F the sum, in
	/// their ORIGINAL directions, of the ones it turned round — so
	/// U = (raw + folded)/2 and F = (raw - folded)/2, both exact. The voxel's
	/// normal is the LARGER of the two, which is the majority group's mean, which
	/// is what the pin stored (it dropped the opposing triangles from the sum and
	/// divided by the whole count); the double-sided flag is "the other group is
	/// not empty", which is what the pin's exclusion meant. No threshold, no
	/// epsilon: both tests are integer comparisons of the two sums.
	///
	/// The one place this is NOT the pin's answer: upstream dropped a triangle at
	/// more than 120 degrees from the RUNNING sum, while the fold separates at 90,
	/// so a voxel whose normals span 90 to 120 degrees is two groups here and one
	/// there. Measured on gi.field_follows' sub-voxel walls that is worth under
	/// 3 % of the far bounce — the price of not asking the question one triangle
	/// at a time, and of the answer no longer depending on which triangle a thread
	/// reached first.
	INLINE float3 voxelMergeCanonical( float3 n )
	{
		float3 a = abs( n );
		float s = (a.x >= a.y && a.x >= a.z) ? n.x : ((a.y >= a.z) ? n.y : n.z);
		return s < 0.0f ? -n : n;
	}

	/// A contribution in [0, VOXEL_MERGE_CLAMP], on the grid, rounded to nearest.
	INLINE uint4 voxelMergeQuantize4( float4 v )
	{
		return uint4( clamp( v, 0.0f, VOXEL_MERGE_CLAMP ) * VOXEL_MERGE_SCALE + 0.5f );
	}
	INLINE uint3 voxelMergeQuantize3( float3 v )
	{
		return uint3( clamp( v, 0.0f, VOXEL_MERGE_CLAMP ) * VOXEL_MERGE_SCALE + 0.5f );
	}
	/// ...added so it cannot wrap. See THE GRID above: the bound is one
	/// contribution of headroom, and the triangle count is not capped at 65,535
	/// on this backend. The signed NORMAL sums need no such guard — a unit
	/// vector's component is at most 4,096, so they overflow at half a million
	/// triangles in one voxel rather than at sixty-five thousand.
	INLINE uint voxelMergeAddSat( uint sum, uint x )
	{
		return ( sum > ( 0xFFFFFFFFu - x ) ) ? 0xFFFFFFFFu : ( sum + x );
	}
	/// A unit vector's component, on the same grid, as two's complement. `floor`
	/// and not a cast: a cast truncates towards zero and would round -0.5 the
	/// wrong way.
	INLINE int3 voxelMergeQuantizeSigned3( float3 v )
	{
		return int3( floor( clamp( v, -1.0f, 1.0f ) * VOXEL_MERGE_SCALE + 0.5f ) );
	}

	/// THE COVERAGE (PHOTON-VOXEL-3). THE STORE IS A DENSITY FIELD: a voxel a
	/// surface passes through holds that surface's radiance times the FRACTION OF
	/// THE VOXEL IT FILLS - the area of the surface inside the voxel over the
	/// voxel's face area - so a wall's voxels, summed, hold the wall's radiance
	/// times its area, and the opacity the cones composite follows the same rule.
	/// Upstream stored the MEAN over the triangles that touched a voxel, so a
	/// voxel a wall grazed held the wall's full radiance and full opacity: a thin
	/// wall on a cell boundary was stored three voxels thick and every wall was
	/// rounded out to whole cells at its edges (PHOTON-FIELD-ROTATE-1 measured
	/// 1.32-1.44x the authored emission on a one-voxel wall).
	///
	/// A triangle's contribution is its value times its weight w (the Voxelizer's
	/// clipped area over the face area), both on this grid: the product of two
	/// 12-bit fixed-point numbers, rounded back to 12 bits - an exact integer
	/// operation, so the sums stay order-independent. A triangle's piece of a
	/// voxel is at most the cube's largest cross-section (sqrt 2), and w is
	/// clamped to 2: the product of a clamped contribution (65,536) and a weight
	/// (8,192) is 2^29, inside a uint.
	INLINE uint voxelMergeWeight( float w )
	{
		return uint( clamp( w, 0.0f, 2.0f ) * VOXEL_MERGE_SCALE + 0.5f );
	}
	INLINE uint4 voxelMergeWeigh4( uint4 q, uint w )
	{
		return ( q * w + 2048u ) >> 12u;
	}
	INLINE uint3 voxelMergeWeigh3( uint3 q, uint w )
	{
		return ( q * w + 2048u ) >> 12u;
	}
	/// Signed: the arithmetic shift rounds towards minus infinity on both signs,
	/// the same rule as the quantisation's floor.
	INLINE int3 voxelMergeWeighSigned3( int3 q, uint w )
	{
		return ( q * int( w ) + 2048 ) >> 12;
	}


	/// THE RESOLVE, FROM THE SUMS. Every dispatch that adds anything to a voxel
	/// writes this afterwards, from the TOTAL the accumulator then holds — so
	/// whichever dispatch happens to be last leaves the complete answer, and an
	/// earlier one's partial answer is always overwritten by it. That is why the
	/// resolve rides the voxelisation instead of being a pass of its own: the
	/// value written does not depend on WHO writes it, only on the sums, and the
	/// sums are exact.
	///
	/// THE DIVISOR IS THE AREA (PHOTON-VOXEL-3). `totalWeight` is the voxel's
	/// isotropic coverage W on the grid (the sum of the pieces' area weights). Every
	/// MATERIAL PROPERTY of the voxel - its albedo, its emissive radiance, its normal,
	/// its roughness - is the area-weighted MEAN, sum / W: what the surfaces inside the
	/// voxel ARE, per unit of their area. How MUCH of the voxel they fill is not a
	/// material property and is not folded into any of them: it is the per-axis
	/// coverage (THE DIRECTIONAL COVERAGE, below), and its largest component rides in
	/// the albedo's alpha as the voxel's conservative opacity c = max( O_x, O_y, O_z ).
	/// The light injection multiplies what the voxel emits by c (the light volume is
	/// premultiplied by its alpha: the mips average colour and alpha together, the
	/// ray hit divides colour by alpha and gets the mean radiance back), and the
	/// reader's mip-0 read replaces c by the opacity along the ray.
	INLINE float voxelMergeInvMean( uint totalWeight )
	{
		return 1.0f / float( max( totalWeight, 1u ) );
	}
	INLINE float4 voxelMergeAlbedo( uint4 sumAlbedo, uint totalWeight, float3 coverage )
	{
		return float4( float3( sumAlbedo.xyz ) * voxelMergeInvMean( totalWeight ),
					   max( coverage.x, max( coverage.y, coverage.z ) ) );
	}

	/// THE DIRECTIONAL COVERAGE (PHOTON-VOXEL-3). A ray along axis a sees of a
	/// voxel the area of its surfaces PROJECTED onto the plane normal to a, over the
	/// voxel's face area, times their opacity: O_a = sum of w * alpha * |n . a| over
	/// the pieces (w = the piece's area over the face area, n its geometric normal).
	/// A wall on the xy plane fills its voxels along z (O_z = 1) and is invisible
	/// along x and y (O_x = O_y = 0), which is what makes a thin closed box right:
	/// its side faces are opaque along their own normals and transparent across
	/// the wall. A single per-voxel opacity cannot say both (PHOTON-VOXEL-3 run 1
	/// measured it: an isotropic coverage left a closed wall's edge columns opaque at
	/// full radiance, +31 % over the authored wall).
	///
	/// SPLIT BY THE SIDE A FACE LOOKS TO (PHOTON-VOXEL-4): O_a+ = sum w alpha max( n . a, 0 ),
	/// O_a- = sum w alpha max( -n . a, 0 ). A ray travelling +a meets the faces looking back
	/// at it, O_a-; a solid's back face is never read and a closed wall is read ONCE at any
	/// cell size. Summed over both sides into one value, a wall's front and back added once
	/// the cell resolved them: the open floor's wall cone read 0.49 / 0.85 / 0.87 / 0.89 of a
	/// reference 0.413 at 32 / 64 / 128 / 256 cells, split 0.35 / 0.36 / 0.37 / 0.38
	/// (spikes/photon-voxel-4/lab, split-run.txt). A one-sided surface is invisible from
	/// behind - the reader's backface rule; a two-sided sheet carries both sides' pieces.
	///
	/// THE GRID. 1/512 per axis, the three sums packed 11/11/10 bits into ONE
	/// accumulator texel (x in bits 0..10, y in 11..21, z in 22..31): 4.0, 4.0 and
	/// 2.0 of full coverage before a field saturates, far above the resolve's cap of
	/// 1. Each field is added with its OWN saturation, which is commutative and
	/// associative, so the merge stays exactly as order-independent as the other
	/// fourteen sums. One contribution is rounded to nearest (+-1/1024) and at most
	/// sqrt 2 (the cube's largest cross-section), 724 steps.
	INLINE uint3 voxelMergeAxisQuantize( float3 v )
	{
		return uint3( clamp( v, 0.0f, 2.0f ) * 512.0f + 0.5f );
	}
	INLINE uint3 voxelMergeAxisUnpack( uint packed )
	{
		return uint3( packed & 0x7FFu, ( packed >> 11u ) & 0x7FFu, packed >> 22u );
	}
	INLINE uint voxelMergeAxisAddSat( uint packed, uint3 add )
	{
		uint3 sum = min( voxelMergeAxisUnpack( packed ) + min( add, uint3( 0x7FFu, 0x7FFu, 0x3FFu ) ),
						 uint3( 0x7FFu, 0x7FFu, 0x3FFu ) );
		return sum.x | ( sum.y << 11u ) | ( sum.z << 22u );
	}
	/// THE POSITION TAKES WHAT THE COVERAGE TAKES (PHOTON-VOXEL-5; the VOXEL-4 audit's F2).
	/// The coverage fields saturate at 4.0 / 4.0 / 2.0 (x / y / z) of full coverage; a
	/// position sum added whole past that point divided an unsaturated numerator by a
	/// saturated denominator - three full layers along z at 0.1 / 0.5 / 0.9 of one cell read
	/// the mean at 0.75, not 0.5. So each field's position add is scaled by the share of its
	/// coverage add the field accepted: the mean of what the store holds is the mean of the
	/// contributions it counted (exact within one batch; across batches the first batches
	/// fill the field - the batches run in a fixed order, so the store is deterministic).
	/// Past the cap a voxel is opaque along that axis at any count (the resolve caps O at
	/// 1), and only the position of the counted surfaces is kept.
	INLINE void voxelMergeAxisPosAddSat( OGRE_INOUT_REF( uint, packedAxis ),
										  OGRE_INOUT_REF( uint, packedPos ), uint3 addAxis,
										  uint3 addPos )
	{
		const uint3 cap = uint3( 0x7FFu, 0x7FFu, 0x3FFu );
		const uint3 before = voxelMergeAxisUnpack( packedAxis );
		const uint3 after = min( before + min( addAxis, cap ), cap );
		const uint3 taken = after - before;
		uint3 posAdd = uint3( 0u, 0u, 0u );
		for( int a = 0; a < 3; ++a )
		{
			if( addAxis[a] > 0u )
				posAdd[a] = ( addPos[a] * taken[a] + ( addAxis[a] >> 1u ) ) / addAxis[a];
		}
		packedAxis = after.x | ( after.y << 11u ) | ( after.z << 22u );
		packedPos = voxelMergeAxisAddSat( packedPos, posAdd );
	}
	/// O_a = min( 1, S_a ): a voxel cannot be more than opaque along an axis (the
	/// cap bites where two surfaces project onto the same face - a closed box's
	/// front and back in one cell - and nowhere on a single surface).
	INLINE float3 voxelMergeAxisResolve( uint packed )
	{
		return min( float3( voxelMergeAxisUnpack( packed ) ) * ( 1.0f / 512.0f ),
					float3( 1.0f, 1.0f, 1.0f ) );
	}
	/// THE SURFACE POSITION (PHOTON-VOXEL-4). Per axis a, where along a the surfaces a ray
	/// along a meets in this voxel lie: the coverage-weighted mean of the pieces' centroids
	/// along a, p_a = sum w alpha |n_a| c_a / sum w alpha |n_a| - the second packed sum
	/// holds sum( coverage contribution x c_a ) with c_a the piece's centroid in the voxel's
	/// own cell coordinate [0, 1], on the coverage's 1/512 grid (the same packing and the
	/// same per-field saturation; c_a <= 1, so it never saturates before the coverage).
	/// Stored ABSOLUTE in the volume's normalised coordinate and O-PREMULTIPLIED, pO_a =
	/// O_a x ( ( cell_a + p_a ) / res_a ), RGBA16_UNORM: a box filter and a trilinear fetch
	/// then give sum O p / sum O - the coverage-weighted mean position exactly, at every
	/// mip (a texel with O = 0 has no say). A voxel no surface reaches stores 0.
	INLINE float3 voxelMergePositionResolve( uint packedPos, uint packedAxis, uint3 voxelCel,
											 float3 invRes )
	{
		float3 sumAxis = float3( voxelMergeAxisUnpack( packedAxis ) );
		float3 sumPos = float3( voxelMergeAxisUnpack( packedPos ) );
		float3 local = float3( 0.5f, 0.5f, 0.5f );
		for( int a = 0; a < 3; ++a )
		{
			if( sumAxis[a] > 0.0f )
				local[a] = min( sumPos[a] / sumAxis[a], 1.0f );
		}
		return voxelMergeAxisResolve( packedAxis ) * ( ( float3( voxelCel ) + local ) * invRes );
	}

	INLINE float4 voxelMergeNormal( uint4 sumNormal, uint4 sumFolded, uint totalWeight )
	{
		float invAccum = voxelMergeInvMean( totalWeight );
		// THE TWO GROUPS, RECOVERED. Write the folded sum as U - F and the raw one
		// as U + F, where U is the sum of the normals the fold left alone and F
		// the sum, IN THEIR ORIGINAL DIRECTIONS, of the ones it turned round: then
		// U = (raw + folded)/2 and F = (raw - folded)/2, both exact.
		int3 iRaw	= int3( sumNormal.xyz );
		int3 iFolded	= int3( sumFolded.xyz );
		int3 iU		= iRaw + iFolded;
		int3 iF		= iRaw - iFolded;
		bool emptyU	= ( iU.x == 0 ) && ( iU.y == 0 ) && ( iU.z == 0 );
		bool emptyF	= ( iF.x == 0 ) && ( iF.y == 0 ) && ( iF.z == 0 );

		float3 nU = float3( iU ) * ( 0.5f * invAccum );
		float3 nF = float3( iF ) * ( 0.5f * invAccum );

		// AND THE DECISION IS GEOMETRY, NOT MEMBERSHIP. "Both groups are
		// non-empty" is NOT the question: the fold's seam runs along the six arcs
		// where the largest component changes (x = -y, x = -z, y = -z), and a
		// smooth surface crosses those arcs all the time — (0.72, 0, -0.69) and
		// (0.69, 0, -0.72) are 6.5 degrees apart and land one in each group. A
		// membership test calls that voxel double-sided, and the injection then
		// takes abs(NdotL) and lights it FROM BEHIND: the leak class, on every
		// sphere, cylinder, character and curved wall (about 5 % of the surface
		// voxels of a 32-voxel sphere), and on flat walls built from several
		// instances whose matrices differ in the last bit.
		//
		// So ask the pin's own question of the two group MEANS instead: are they
		// more than 120 degrees apart? `dot(U, F) < -0.5 |U| |F|`, compared
		// squared to keep it to one multiply and no roots. The seam pair reads
		// dot > 0 and is one surface; a plate thinner than a voxel reads
		// dot = -|U||F| and is two. Both groups' sums are exact integers, so the
		// answer is still order-independent.
		float dUF	= dot( nU, nF );
		float lenU2	= dot( nU, nU );
		float lenF2	= dot( nF, nF );
		bool doubleSided = !emptyU && !emptyF && dUF < 0.0f &&
						   ( dUF * dUF ) > ( 0.25f * lenU2 * lenF2 );

		// WHAT THE VOXEL'S NORMAL IS. One surface: the RAW mean, U + F, which is
		// exactly what the pin stored when its 120-degree test excluded nothing.
		// Two surfaces: the majority group's mean, which is what the pin's
		// exclusion left behind (it dropped the opposing triangles and divided by
		// the whole count).
		//
		// Taking the LARGER of the two and not U is load-bearing: a voxel whose
		// normals ALL lie in the half-space the fold turns round (a wall facing
		// -X, say) has U = 0, and storing that would leave it black. Measured: it
		// costs gi.leak_room four assertions.
		//
		// RESIDUAL, and it is the mirror of the seam: two normals about 170
		// degrees apart CAN both land in U (their largest components on different
		// axes, both positive), and then F is empty, the raw mean cancels and the
		// voxel is stored with a near-zero normal instead of the pin's majority
		// cluster. It needs the pair to straddle the |x| = |y| tie within about a
		// degree, and the answer it gives — a voxel that receives almost no light
		// — is the dark side, not the leaking one.
		float3 normalOut = nU + nF;
		if( doubleSided )
		{
			normalOut = lenU2 >= lenF2 ? nU : nF;
			// A TWO-SIDED VOXEL'S NORMAL IS CANONICAL (PHOTON-VOXEL-5): the light is stored per SIDE
			// (LightInjection: the front, the side this normal points to, and the back), and a read
			// FILTERS the two sides' volumes across neighbouring voxels - so neighbours must agree
			// which physical side is the front. The majority group flips between voxels of one slab
			// on a tie (equal faces both sides), so the side is fixed by a generic direction instead:
			// the front is the one facing it. A slab whose normal is perpendicular to it sits on its
			// SEAM and may flip between neighbours, so no normal content favours may lie there: the
			// direction maximises the angle from the seam to all 26 vectors with components in
			// {-1, 0, 1} (the axes, the face diagonals, the body diagonals) - each is 9.9 degrees or
			// more off it; the nearest integer normals with components up to 2 are 1.5 degrees off,
			// up to 5 are 0.19 degrees off. (The earlier (1,2,3)/sqrt(14) had the body diagonal
			// (1,1,-1) exactly on its seam: a slab tilted so could flip voxel by voxel.)
			if( dot( normalOut, float3( 0.8925f, 0.4166f, 0.1726f ) ) < 0.0f )
				normalOut = -normalOut;
		}

		// Stored unnormalized and biased, exactly as before, so the injection's
		// `xyz * 2 - 1` keeps meaning what it meant; .a is the double-sided flag
		// (the injection lights both sides of such a voxel).
		return float4( normalOut * 0.5f + 0.5f, doubleSided ? 1.0f : 0.0f );
	}
	// Jahshaka (PHOTON-WRITER-1): .w carries the voxel's MEAN ROUGHNESS (the
	// fourteenth sum, the materials' perceptual roughness per triangle), which the
	// light injection and the bounce read to make the voxel emit what its surface
	// renders. Nothing read the old constant 0 in .w.
	INLINE float4 voxelMergeEmissive( uint4 sumEmissive, uint sumRoughness, uint totalWeight )
	{
		return float4( float3( sumEmissive.xyz ) * voxelMergeInvMean( totalWeight ),
					   float( sumRoughness ) * voxelMergeInvMean( totalWeight ) );
	}

@end
